[Music]  [Applause]  this debate  is not new  it's been going on for all of my  activist life  but i'd say that  the cancer culture moment  is much more pronounced today than it  was say 30 or 40 years ago  there's always been a dilemma  within  free speech  about what limits there should be if any  where do you draw the line  do people have a right to say whatever  they want  in theory in a democracy they certainly  should  but obviously if you're inciting  violence  if you're making false damaging  allegations against people that are  untrue and and harmful  if you're engaging in threats menaces of  harassment then that is not legitimate  free speech  now  in the past  i have been involved in  quite a few  council culture campaigns  in the 1970s  i was involved in the anti-nazi league  opposing the neo-nazi national front  which conducted  quasi-terror campaigns against black and  asian communities and i thought that  that was absolutely right to no platform  those extreme fascists who were  threatening and menacing our minority  communities  in the 1990s and the earlier part of the  century i was involved in many campaigns  to cancel  islamist preachers  who have been given invitations to speak  at our universities  even while they were saying things like  that men and women should be segregated  saying that  jews deserve to be killed  that  sufi muslims or shia muslims depending  on which sect they were involved in  should die that they were apostates they  were heretics heretics  um saying that lgbt plus people and  women who have sex outside of marriage  should be executed  and i make no apology  for  supporting their cancellation  because of the  ideas they're expressing which are  beyond bigotry beyond hatred but  actually advocating endorsing glorifying  violence  i think there are possibly  three  red lines  where free speech can be legitimately  restricted  one is making false damaging allegations  such as someone as a rapist a tax  fraudster a paedophile and so on  um  when they make  actions or words that amount to  harassment menaces threats and  intimidation  and when there are incitements to  violence  those are  i think not legitimate free speech  grounds  but of course  today's council culture  often goes way beyond those limited  grounds in terms of deciding who  can speak and who can't  it is important to hold people to  account  if people hold hateful or bigoted views  they should be challenged they should be  protested against but i think that in  terms of limiting their right to speak  denying them the right to speak that is  you know a step  that is i think being interpreted much  more widely than say 30 or 40 years ago  now cancer culture is often described as  a problem or a crime of the left  but it's equally  one of the right many of you all  familiar with the recent example of gita  hari  on gb news  who took the knee  in solidarity with black lives matter  and was cancelled by gb news you know a  right-wing news outlet  so  it isn't simply  a left issue or a woke culture issue  what is interesting and i think perhaps  disturbing about cancer culture  as it is often practiced  is that it frequently involves  misrepresentation  or distortion  and fabrication  so for example  i was denounced and there were attempts  to cancel me on the grounds of racism  because i was involved in a campaign  called stop murder music  this is a campaign against eight  jamaican reggae and dancehall singers  who were advocating the murder of lgbt  plus people in jamaica  and corresponding with their music there  was a huge spike in vine attacks and the  killings of lgbt plus people in jamaica  i was told by some black activists that  this was a racist campaign against black  people black music and against jamaica  it clearly was nothing of the sort the  race of the singers was irrelevant it  was their incitements to murder which  was the issue  uh likewise  when i called out islamic extremists who  are saying that women  who  defy  traditional islamic laws and  moral codes  should be beaten  should expect to be killed by their  families  and indeed those who have sex outside of  marriage  should be executed  i was told that was islamophobic  that i was  colluding with an anti-muslim witch hunt  well again  there was no demonization of muslim  people per se  it was targeted narrowly and exclusively  at those who advocated these violent  crimes  against other people and in particular  against other muslims  the shocking thing is the prime victims  of islamist extremism  are not white people like me but fellow  muslims and i have dealt with many many  cases here in britain of  honour killings or attempted on killings  of muslims  by their own families or by extremists  the left  and i say this with some regret as a  left wing of myself  the left is often silenced about this  indeed not only silent but when people  like me  champion these causes  we are denounced as  islamophobic and again there were  attempts unsuccessful  to cancel me on account of that  another example is some years ago i  signed a letter  condemning violent attacks  and  threats in the debate about trans issues  and women's rights  the letter simply said that it's wrong  to resort to violence that these debates  these discussions these disagreements  needed to be dealt with in a civil way  there was nothing in that letter that  said a word against trans people  yet because i signed that letter  i was deemed to be transphobic and  colluding with transphobes and again  there were attempts  to cancel me  now none of these attempts  have ever succeeded  so i'm not playing the victim here you  know i fought off those attempts but  others have not been so lucky i think of  the iranian feminist and communist  mariam namazi  who has had not only attempts but  successful attempts to stop her from  speaking because she's spoken out for  women's rights  against islamist extremists in defense  of muslim women  a culture she herself comes from  another thing is that  a lot of the cancer culture  often ignores the intention  [Music]  and the context  so  people may say things  that are mistaken or wrong  but it's clear from their history and  background that they never intended to  say that  so for example sometimes people will use  the wrong pronouns  not because they're trying to misgender  people  or deny their gender identity but just  because they didn't realize the person  was trans or non-binary or gender fluid  but they will often be called out and  denounced  in a very very harsh unforgiving way  because of a genuine mistake if you look  at context  i can remember  in the 1990s when the lgbt plus group  outrage that i was involved in  sought to reclaim the word queer  it had traditionally been used as a term  of abuse and a put down  but we sought to reclaim it  as a symbol of pride and affirmation  but often when we  used the word queer  in a positive context we were still  denounced as colluding with homophobia  now quite clearly if someone calls me an  effing queer  that is a derogatory insulting and  homophobic intention  if i talk about defending queer human  rights  celebrating queer culture i'm affirming  i'm celebrating i'm supporting that  so  when we look at what people say  the context in which they're saying it  and the intention is so important  but often when it comes to cancer  culture those factors are ignored  often there's also  a rush to judgment  to continue watching this video click  the link in the top left or in the  description below or visit iai.tv for  more debates and talks from the world's  leading thinkers on today's biggest  ideas  you

